// The refusals around `Disp-Key` (@PLN162 step 6), each pinned by its message — the pass-2
// ones; the same types twice is a pass-1 refusal that stops the rest, and has its own file.
// A free definition is not a method, so the method spelling does not reach it; a call no definition takes is refused naming what was
// passed and what is declared (`Disp-Exhaustive`); two definitions that both take the call —
// a defaulted trailing parameter beside a shorter one — are refused as ambiguous, naming
// both (`Disp-Ambiguous`); and a multi-definition name offers no parse hint, so a
// width-carrying literal argument is refused where a typed local is the cure (`Disp-Hint`).
// @falsified-at: 73f6cdc67 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/4 -> 0/4 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/4 -> 0/4 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-name-with-several-definitions-refuses-what-is-not-an-overload.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/4 -> 0/4 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/4 -> 0/4 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
//   CHANNEL: expectations — none of the four `@EXPECT_ERROR` lines is met on the control,
//   which refuses the program earlier and differently (*Cannot redefine 'hit'* at the second
//   definition), so the suite's verdict is FAIL there and the file's exit goes 1 -> 0 with it.
//   Not leak and not assert: this whole file is a parse-time verdict.
//   ARMED: no instrument — the refusals are parse-time on either backend.
//   WITNESS: on the control the diagnostic is *Cannot redefine 'hit'*; here it is the four
//   named ones and nothing else — the runner fails an unexpected error.
//   HOLDS: no diagnostic beyond the four named ones on this tree, and the leak channel is
//   `none` on both and does not move.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Unknown field Fire.hit
// @EXPECT_ERROR: no definition of `hit` takes (Rock) — declared: hit(Fire), hit(Ice)
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `amb(Fire)` is ambiguous — it is taken by amb(Fire) and amb(Fire, integer)
// @EXPECT_ERROR: got vector<integer> on argument 2 of call to take

struct Fire { n: integer }
struct Ice { n: integer }
struct Rock { n: integer }

fn hit(f: Fire) -> text { "fire {f.n}" }
fn hit(i: Ice) -> text { "ice {i.n}" }

fn amb(f: Fire) -> text { "one {f.n}" }
fn amb(f: Fire, k: integer = 0) -> text { "two {f.n} {k}" }

fn take(f: Fire, xs: vector<u8>) -> integer { f.n + len(xs) }
fn take(i: Ice, xs: vector<u8>) -> integer { i.n + len(xs) }

fn main() {
  f = Fire { n: 1 };
  f.hit();
  hit(Rock { n: 2 });
  amb(f);
  take(f, [1, 2]);
}
